3
00:00:26,279 --> 00:00:34,990
JAMIE: So the government's basically saying energy risk is no longer a tail event, it's the structural cost of doing business in America for the next three years.

4
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:49,303
MAYA: Apple's new CEO just dusted off a twenty-five-year-old Steve Jobs playbook—the one where you stand on stage and tell people the iPhone is still the thing, and somehow that counts as innovation. We've gone from inventing the future to reminding people the past still works.

5
00:00:49,303 --> 00:01:01,126
MAYA: Automattic's board finally did what boards are supposed to do—they told Matt Mullenweg to take a leave of absence. Against his will. That's the sentence that kills the founder-worship narrative: when the board wins, the founder loses.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/09/automattics-board-forces-ceo-matt-mullenweg-into-leave-of-absence/

6
00:01:01,126 --> 00:01:10,460
ALEX: Mullenweg announced it on Slack like it was his choice, but governance doesn't work that way. The board forced him out. That's not a sabbatical, that's a legal ejection.

7
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:19,172
MAYA: AI deepfakes are now good enough that voters can't tell what's real anymore—which means the election just became a trust exercise in institutions we've already stopped trusting.

8
00:01:19,172 --> 00:01:30,373
JAMIE: So we've gone from 'fake news' as a political slur to actual fake videos flooding the airwaves, and the only thing standing between democracy and total epistemic collapse is your ability to spot a deepfake.

9
00:01:30,373 --> 00:01:37,218
ALEX: Three years from now, when that diesel bill hits your door, you'll remember we all saw it coming and did nothing.

10
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:42,196
MAYA: The forecast was the surrender—we just didn't call it that until it was too late.

11
00:01:42,196 --> 00:01:46,552
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
